\title{Common-neighbour counts and the Clebsch graph}
\author{Human Compression project}
\date{13 October 2026}
\begin{document}\maketitle
\begin{abstract}
We recover the uniqueness of the sixteen-vertex graph of degree five
and girth four having four shortest cycles through each edge. The
second moment of the common-neighbour counts forces every pair of
nonadjacent vertices to have two common neighbours. The complete
adjacency structure then follows from the two-element subsets of
a five-element set. No census of regular or strongly regular graphs
is required.
\end{abstract}

\section{The source entry and its scope}
All graphs here are finite, simple and undirected. An
$\operatorname{egr}(n,k,g,\lambda)$ is a connected graph on $n$
vertices of degree $k$ and girth $g$, with precisely $\lambda$
cycles of length $g$ through every edge. Table~3 of the fixed edition
\nolinkurl{arXiv:2401.08271v2} of Goedgebeur and Jooken~\cite{GJ}
states that the minimum order for $(k,g,\lambda)=(5,4,4)$ is sixteen,
with exactly one graph at that order. The earlier lower bound is
attributed there to reference~[8, Theorem~2.3]. Our previously
reviewed components already establish that bound and the existence
of the source example \nolinkurl{UntilOrder250_51}.

The graph and its uniqueness are classical, and are not claimed as
new results. The elementary second-moment argument below is included
in full, without a priority claim. The contribution of this note is
the explicit recovery of the source's remaining uniqueness entry
and its checked identification with the stored example. It supplies
no solution of an open conjecture.

\section{A moment bound and equality}
\begin{lemma}\label{moment}
Let $X$ be triangle-free, regular of degree $k\ge2$, and suppose
every edge lies on exactly $\lambda$ four-cycles. For a fixed
vertex $v$, put
\[
 W=V(X)\setminus(\{v\}\cup N(v)),\qquad
 m_x=|N(x)\cap N(v)|\quad(x\in W).
\]
Then
\begin{equation}\label{moments}
 \sum_{x\in W}m_x=k(k-1),\qquad
 \sum_{x\in W}m_x^2=k(k-1+\lambda).
\end{equation}
In particular,
\begin{equation}\label{bound}
 |V(X)|\ge 1+k+\frac{k(k-1)^2}{k-1+\lambda}.
\end{equation}
If equality holds, all the $m_x$ are equal.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
There are $k(k-1)$ paths of length two starting at $v$ without
immediately returning to it. Their endpoints lie in $W$, because
there are no triangles. Counting them by endpoint gives the first
identity. Each choice of two common neighbours of $v,x$ gives
one four-cycle through $v$, so the number of such cycles is
$\sum_{x\in W}\binom{m_x}{2}$. On the other hand, counting them
through the $k$ edges at $v$ counts each cycle twice, giving
$k\lambda/2$. Hence
\[
 \sum_{x\in W}m_x^2
 =\sum_{x\in W}m_x+2\sum_{x\in W}\binom{m_x}{2}
 =k(k-1)+k\lambda.
\]
The Cauchy--Schwarz inequality applied to the vectors
$(m_x)_{x\in W}$ and $(1)_{x\in W}$ gives
\[
 k^2(k-1)^2\le |W|\,k(k-1+\lambda).
\]
As $|W|=|V(X)|-k-1$, this proves \eqref{bound}. Equality in
Cauchy--Schwarz says precisely that the $m_x$ are constant.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}\label{classification}
The least order of an $\operatorname{egr}(n,5,4,4)$ is sixteen,
and there is exactly one graph at that order.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Lemma~\ref{moment} gives $n\ge1+5+5\cdot16/8=16$.
At $n=16$, equality holds at every root $v$. There are ten
vertices in $W$, and their $m_x$ sum to twenty, so $m_x=2$
for each of them. Thus every two nonadjacent vertices have
exactly two common neighbours.

Fix $v$, and label its five neighbours by $1,\ldots,5$.
Each of the ten remaining vertices is adjacent to a two-element
subset of these labels. Each pair of labels has exactly two
common neighbours, one of which is $v$, so there is exactly
one remaining vertex for each pair. Write it as $b_{ij}$.

The vertex $b_{ij}$ has its two neighbours $i,j$ among the
labels. It has no edge to $v$. An edge to another pair vertex
can occur only for disjoint pairs, since overlapping pairs
have a shared label and would form a triangle. There are
exactly three pairs disjoint from $\{i,j\}$, namely the
two-element subsets of the other three labels. Degree five
forces adjacency to all three. Every edge is now determined,
so there is at most one graph at equality.

For existence, use the following sixteen vertices: a vertex
$*$, five vertices $a_i$ indexed by $i\in\{1,\ldots,5\}$,
and ten vertices $b_{ij}$ indexed by unordered pairs. Join
$*$ to all $a_i$, join $a_i$ to the four pair vertices containing
$i$, and join two pair vertices exactly when their pairs are
disjoint. These rules give degree five at every vertex, and
the graph is connected through $*$ and the label vertices.

There is no triangle. A triangle containing $*$ would require
an edge between two labels. A triangle containing $a_i$ and
two pair vertices would require two disjoint pairs both
containing $i$. A triangle of three pair vertices would require
three disjoint pairs inside a five-element set. All three
possibilities are impossible.

Every nonadjacent pair of distinct vertices has two common
neighbours, as the following complete list shows:
\begin{itemize}
\item $*$ and $b_{ij}$ have common neighbours $a_i,a_j$.
\item $a_i,a_j$ with $i\ne j$ have common neighbours $*,b_{ij}$.
\item $a_i,b_{jk}$ with $i\notin\{j,k\}$ have common neighbours
$b_{i\ell}$, where $\ell$ ranges over the two labels outside
$\{i,j,k\}$.
\item $b_{ij},b_{ik}$ with $j\ne k$ have common neighbours $a_i$
and $b_{\ell m}$, where $\{\ell,m\}$ is the complement of
$\{i,j,k\}$ in the five labels.
\end{itemize}
For an edge $pq$ and any $s\in N(p)\setminus\{q\}$, the
vertices $s,q$ are nonadjacent and have two common neighbours.
One is $p$; the other completes a unique four-cycle through
$pq$ with second neighbour $s$ at $p$. The four choices of
$s$ yield precisely four such cycles. Therefore the graph has
girth four and the required parameters, proving existence
and Theorem~\ref{classification}. This is the Clebsch graph.
\end{proof}

\paragraph{Certificates and limits.}
The accompanying verifier constructs the displayed sixteen-vertex
graph. It counts four-cycles both by paths and by four-vertex
subsets, checks the two moment identities at every vertex, and
verifies a literal bijection to the acquired source example.
The written argument supplies completeness. The newly recovered
table entry is uniqueness only: the lower bound and existence
retain their earlier credit. The note does not complete all
source tables, add a compression measurement, or provide a
new Lean formalization.

